# Precise Wording = Truth

Do not use completion language unless the claim is mechanically proven in this session.

Forbidden without evidence: `done`, `clean`, `shipped`, `pushed`, `deployed`, `online`, `implemented`, `fixed`, `verified`, `safe`, `set in stone`.

Use exact status wording:

- `local draft`: files changed but not committed.
- `committed locally`: commit exists locally but is not proven on `origin/main`.
- `pushed to origin/main`: `HEAD == origin/main` after fetch.
- `deployed`: production deploy SHA equals `origin/main`.
- `verified`: named checks ran and passed in this session.

If evidence is missing or a tool is blocked, say:

`I cannot prove this; treat it as not done.`

Before final completion wording, include or derive from `scripts/release-truth.ps1`:

- branch
- `HEAD` SHA
- `origin/main` SHA
- whether `HEAD == origin/main`
- working tree state
- checks run
- deploy SHA when claiming online/deployed

If any required evidence is absent, report the narrower truthful status instead of completion.
